### Title
Search a large sharded channel after an exact-name miss.

### Why
Wildcard searches previously constructed a package record for every name in the
shard index. A typo against `conda-pypi` could spend tens of seconds on the CPU
without showing progress during the fuzzy fallback.

### Platforms
- [ ] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
- Network access to `conda-pypi`.
- Hyperfine to compare the same commands before and after the fix.

### Steps
1. Warm the cache with an exact-name hit:

   ```console
   conda search --override-channels -c conda-pypi --repodata-use-shards ab5
   ```

2. Run the miss in a terminal and check progress during the fuzzy fallback:

   ```console
   conda search --override-channels -c conda-pypi --repodata-use-shards abccc
   ```

3. Compare the two miss paths before and after the fix, using the same machine
   and Python version:

   ```console
   hyperfine --warmup 1 --runs 5 --ignore-failure "conda search --override-channels -c conda-pypi --repodata-use-shards abccc" "conda search --override-channels -c conda-pypi --repodata-use-shards '*abccc*'"
   ```

### Pass criteria
- The exact hit lists `ab5` builds.
- The fuzzy fallback displays progress until the search finishes.
- Both miss paths report that the package was not found, without spending
  tens of seconds on a warmed-cache name scan.
- Hyperfine shows a substantial reduction in the miss-path runtime.

### Out of scope / notes
Missing platform subdirectories can still produce repodata 404s. Automated
tests cover name filtering, selective shard loading, record construction,
and progress while consuming deferred search results.
